hkdfguard (0.9.2-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Secrets: hkdfguard_wrap_secret, hkdfguard_unwrap_secret and
    hkdfguard_open_secret_file wrap a password (1 to 256 bytes) bound to
    a secret name; the CLI's new `protect` command writes one.
  * The plaintext-key check on key files also catches a DEK followed by a
    CRLF line ending.
  * Ephemeral provider: a forked child no longer wraps under the all-zero
    key it inherits; it has no ephemeral keys until it creates its own.
  * A provider that has gone missing while holding a service's KEK (a TPM
    device that disappeared, a pinned external secret not mounted, a
    PKCS#11 session logged out underneath a call) fails the call instead
    of handing it to a weaker provider. Audit event 1006 records each
    service served by a provider later in the policy order.
  * TPM authValue files (<service>.tpmauth) are no longer given the key
    directory's group: root writes each as root:<group> 0440 for the group
    named in the new tpm.auth_groups policy table, or root:root 0400.
    Non-root services need an entry before they are provisioned.
  * Provisioning always creates a new authValue, under a lock on the key
    directory, rather than reusing one left without its key file.
  * With no policy-allowed provider present on the host (a required TPM
    missing, say), wrap and hkdfguard_kek_fingerprint return
    PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE, audited as event 2005, instead of the unaudited
    KEK_NOT_FOUND; hkdfguard_kek_exists returns it instead of answering 0.
  * PKCS#11: a key under a service's label is used only if it is also
    CKA_LOCAL (generated on the token, not derived or unwrapped) and not
    modifiable or copyable, as hkdfguard generates its keys.
  * PKCS#11: every PIN-class C_Login failure (incorrect, invalid, wrong
    length, expired, locked) stops further logins until the PIN file
    changes, keyed to the exact file version the PIN was read from.
  * CLI: wrap --force warns when it may not overwrite the key file it
    replaces, instead of skipping the overwrite silently.
  * Unwrap refuses a payload longer than any wrap produces before parsing
    it, rather than copying it first.
  * The build refuses the release profile with --cfg hkdfguard_test_paths
    (the test harnesses' policy override), so it can't leak into a release.
  * The TPM key directory gets root:hkdfguard 0750 only when it is
    created; an owner or mode an administrator sets survives upgrades.

 -- runsecure <torin@torinblair.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

hkdfguard (0.9.1-1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Pre-release 0.9.1: post-quantum HMAC wrapping (payload format 1).
  * TPM keys carry a per-service authValue (<service>.tpmauth) that keys
    session encryption; tpm.session_encryption is now "on" or "off".
  * A configured provider that is failing no longer hands KEKs to a weaker
    one.
  * wrap --force replaces a key file atomically.
  * TPM key files from 0.9.0 must be provisioned again.

 -- runsecure <torin@torinblair.com>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

